.TH MALLOC 3
.SH NAME
malloc, free, realloc, calloc \- memory allocator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <malloc.h>

char *
malloc(size)
unsigned size;

free(ptr)
char *ptr;

char *
realloc(ptr, size)
char *ptr;
unsigned size;

char *
calloc(nelem, elsize)
unsigned nelem, elsize;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B malloc
returns a pointer to a block of at least
.I size
bytes, aligned for any use.
.PP
.B free
releases a block that malloc, realloc or calloc returned, for use again
by a later call. A NULL pointer is ignored. The block must not be used
after it is freed.
.PP
.B realloc
changes the size of the block at
.I ptr
to
.I size
bytes, and returns a pointer to the block, which may have moved. Its
contents are unchanged, up to the smaller of the old and new sizes. A
block made smaller stays where it is, and the space no longer used is
freed. realloc with a NULL
.I ptr
is the same as malloc.
.PP
.B calloc
returns a block for an array of
.I nelem
elements of
.I elsize
bytes each, with every byte set to zero.
.PP
The allocator keeps a list of the free blocks, in the order of their
addresses, and gives each request the first block large enough for it,
joining blocks as they are freed. When none is large enough, it takes
more memory above the program's data with
.BR sbrk (2).
.PP
A program in the 8086's small model has one segment of 64K bytes for its
data, its stack and the memory it allocates, so what malloc can give
comes to less than 64K altogether, and less again as the program's
data and stack are larger.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
malloc, realloc and calloc return NULL when there is not enough memory.
When realloc returns NULL, the old block is still there, unchanged.
.SH SEE ALSO
brk(2), sbrk(2)
.PP
B. W. Kernighan and D. M. Ritchie,
.IR "The C Programming Language" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1978, section 8.7.
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR malloc ,
.B calloc
and
.B realloc
functions first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX; Version 6 had
.B alloc
and
.BR free .
This allocator follows the one in
.IR "The C Programming Language" .
.SH BUGS
Writing past the end of a block, using a block after freeing it, or
freeing it twice damages the list of free blocks, and the program
usually fails later, at a place that has nothing to do with the mistake.
.PP
calloc does not check that
.I nelem
times
.I elsize
fits in an unsigned.
